=========================================================================

bfinger looks up a person or service by an address of the familiar
user@domain form and tells you what that address currently claims about
itself, with the claim's authenticity checked rather than assumed.

The address resolves at its own domain, which publishes an identity key
and names the overlay host that serves it. The claim itself is a record
held by that overlay, committed to the chain by a small mined token that
carries a hash and nothing else. bfinger reads both, checks that the
record is the one the token commits to, checks both signatures against
keys derived from the identity the record names, checks that the update
follows the one before it and is not expired, and checks the token's
merkle proof against block headers it obtained for itself. Nothing in
that chain asks the reader to trust the host that served the answer: a
host can withhold, but it cannot forge.

The key is pinned on first contact, and every later answer is checked
against that pin. A changed key is refused unless the change is signed by
the key that was pinned. This is the part that makes the tool worth
having: an identity system whose keys can be swapped silently by whoever
serves the lookup is not an identity system.

